prerequisites = Array[String](["ds_queues"])
visualization_scene = ExtResource("2_1p6j0")
concept_text = "A linked list is a chain of NODES, where each node holds a value AND a pointer to the next node. Unlike an array, the nodes are NOT stored in consecutive memory spots — they're scattered, but each node knows where the next one lives.

Each node has two parts:
- value: the data it stores
- next: a pointer (or \"link\") to the next node

Visually, it looks like a chain:

    [5 | →] → [10 | →] → [25 | →] → [42 | None]

The LAST node points to None (nothing) to mark the end of the chain.

Three types:
- Singly linked: each node has one pointer (to the next)
- Doubly linked: each node has TWO pointers (next AND previous)
- Circular linked: the last node points back to the first (no end)

The big trade-off compared to arrays:
- ARRAY: instant access by index (O(1)), but slow insertions/deletions in the middle (O(n))
- LINKED LIST: slow access by position (O(n) — must walk the chain), but fast insertions/deletions at known positions (O(1))

When is a linked list better than an array?
- When you insert or delete a lot in the MIDDLE of the list
- When you don't need random access by index
- When size changes a lot (no need to resize)"

real_world_example = "Think of a treasure hunt. Each clue tells you two things: WHERE the treasure is (the value) and WHERE the next clue is (the next pointer). You follow clue 1, which points to clue 2, which points to clue 3, which points to \"X marks the spot\" (None = end of hunt).

    Clue 1: \"Check the old oak tree\"    →  points to Clue 2
    Clue 2: \"Look under the rock\"         →  points to Clue 3
    Clue 3: \"Open the wooden chest\"       →  None (end!)

You can't skip to Clue 3 directly — you have to follow the chain from the start. Each clue only knows about the next one.

Another real-life example: a train. Each car (node) is connected to the next by a coupling (pointer). The engine is the head of the list. To find a specific car, you walk from car to car — you can't jump straight to it. But if you want to add a new car in the middle, you just attach it to the right two couplings and re-route the chain — fast and easy.

A third example: a music playlist. The current song points to the next song, which points to the next, and so on. To skip songs, you follow the chain. If you want to insert a new song between two existing ones, you just update two pointers — no shifting required.

Why use linked lists at all? Because sometimes you need to add or remove things in the MIDDLE of a list all the time. With an array, every insert in the middle means shifting all the items after it — slow! With a linked list, you just update two pointers — instant."

guided_example = "Let's trace through a tiny linked list, one step at a time.

Imagine we have three nodes: node1 → node2 → node3 → None. The values are 5, 10, 25.

Step 1 — Start at the head (the first node).

    current = \"node1\"

The variable current is just a name for \"where we are right now\" in the chain. We start at node1.

Step 2 — Look at the value of the current node.

    if current == \"node1\":
        print(5)               # value of node1
        current = \"node2\"     # move to next node

We check which node we're at, print its value, then update current to point to the next node.

Step 3 — Continue to the next node.

    if current == \"node2\":
        print(10)              # value of node2
        current = \"node3\"     # move to next node

Same pattern: print, then advance.

Step 4 — Continue to the last node.

    if current == \"node3\":
        print(25)              # value of node3
        current = \"None\"      # move to next... but there is no next

Step 5 — Stop when we reach None.

    if current == \"None\":
        # we're done — exit the loop

Output:
    5
    10
    25

That's the whole idea: START at the head, FOLLOW the next pointers until you reach None. Each step prints one value and advances. No random access — you must walk the chain."

recap_text = "- A linked list is a chain of nodes, each pointing to the next
- Each node has two parts: value (data) and next (pointer)
- The last node points to None (end of the chain)
- Access by position is SLOW (O(n)) — you must walk from the head
- Insertion/deletion at a known position is FAST (O(1)) — just update pointers
- Three types: singly, doubly (next + prev), circular (last points to first)
- Compared to arrays: arrays are fast by index but slow to insert in middle
- Linked lists are slow by index but fast to insert in middle
- Used in: music playlists, undo systems, hash table buckets, OS process lists
- The trade-off is access speed vs insert/delete speed"

challenge_instruction = "Drag the blocks to create a tiny chain of two nodes, then print the first node's value."
challenge_blocks = Array[String](["first = 5", "print(first)"])
correct_sequence = Array[String](["first = 5", "print(first)"])
challenge_hint = "Create a variable to hold the first node's value, then print it. We're starting small — just the head of the chain."
code_template = "head = ___
print(head)"
challenge_code = "head = 10
print(head)"
expected_output = "10"
practice_question = "How do you find a specific item in a singly linked list?"
practice_options = Array[String](["Go directly by index, like an array", "Walk the chain from the head until you find it", "Use a hash function", "Items are stored randomly in memory"])
practice_correct_index = 1
practice_explanation = "Linked lists don't have indexes like arrays. To find an item, you start at the head and follow the next pointers, checking each value as you go, until you either find the target or reach None. This is O(n) time — the more items, the longer it takes."
